= How to Play

This guide covers everything you need to know for your first campaign. If you have never played Bannerlord before, start here. If you have played but want to sharpen your fundamentals, work through each section in order.

== Character Creation

Your first decisions shape your entire campaign. Do not rush this.

=== Choosing a Culture

Your culture determines your starting location, initial bonuses, and which factions consider you kin. For the Exile backstory, two cultures make the most sense: Vlandia and Battania.

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, 1fr),
  [*Trait*], [*Vlandia*], [*Battania*],
  [*Location*], [Western Calradia — rolling hills, coastal plains, and castle-studded heartlands], [Northwest forests — dense woodlands, hidden valleys, and ancient hill forts],
  [*Bonus*], [20% more renown from battles — you climb the ranks faster when you fight], [50% less speed penalty in forests — you move freely where others stumble],
  [*Troops*], [Shock cavalry and crossbowmen — hard-hitting charges and devastating ranged volleys], [Archers and skirmishers — the deadliest ranged infantry in Calradia, backed by swift woodsmen],
  [*Narrative Fit*], [You return to your homeland as an exile — these are your people, your hills, your feud], [You are an outsider earning trust among a proud, independent people who value proven skill over bloodlines],
)

If you choose Vlandia, you start in familiar territory — the map you remember from childhood, the lords your father once dined with. But your uncle still rules here, and you must tread carefully. If you choose Battania, you are a stranger in a strange land. This makes the early game harder but aligns with the Exile's journey of starting from nothing and earning every scrap of respect. Both choices are valid. Neither locks you out of any content later.

=== Background Skills

The background selection screen determines your starting skills. There is no "wrong" choice, but some synergize better with the Exile story than others.

Your character spent two years wandering, trading scraps, and fighting to survive. Favor backgrounds that grant *One-Handed* (Aldric's combat training), *Athletics* (you walked across half a continent), *Trade* (you learned to barter or starve), *Leadership* (you had to convince people to trust a stranger), and *Tactics* (every fight you took was one you had to plan). These five skills form the core of a self-made survivor.

Avoid *Roguery*, *Engineering*, and *Steward* early. Roguery requires raiding and criminal activity — you are not a bandit. Engineering and Steward require infrastructure you do not yet have: you need a fief to design, a castle to manage. These skills will become useful in later waves but slow you down at the start.

#tip-box[There is no wrong choice in background selection. Bannerlord is designed so that any combination of skills can succeed. The advice above is about synergy with the Exile narrative and a smoother early game — not a requirement. If you want to play a Roguery-focused exile who turned to crime to survive, that is your story to tell.]

=== Attribute Points

You have limited attribute points at character creation. Each attribute governs three skills, and the number of focus points you can invest in those skills is capped by the attribute's value. Invest wisely — you cannot reset attribute points later.

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Attribute*], [*Governing Skills*],
  [*Vigor (VIG)*], [One-Handed, Two-Handed, Polearm],
  [*Control (CTR)*], [Bow, Crossbow, Throwing],
  [*Endurance (END)*], [Riding, Athletics, Smithing],
  [*Cunning (CNG)*], [Scouting, Tactics, Roguery],
  [*Social (SOC)*], [Charm, Leadership, Trade],
  [*Intelligence (INT)*], [Steward, Medicine, Engineering],
)

For your first character, a balanced spread is safer than min-maxing. Recommended minimums:

- *VIG 3* — enough to fight competently with any melee weapon you pick up
- *END 3* — you will run across battlefields and ride across Calradia; endurance keeps you alive
- *CNG 2-3* — Tactics and Scouting are essential for a party leader; you need them early
- *SOC 2-3* — Trade funds your journey, Leadership grows your warband, Charm opens doors
- *INT 2* — Steward helps with party size, Medicine saves your troops from dying of wounds
- *CTR* is optional — invest here only if you plan a ranged-focused build, such as a horse archer or crossbow captain

#warning-box[You cannot reset attribute points later in the game. Focus points can be shifted through respecialization at the Arena, but attribute points are permanent. A balanced spread with 2-3 in every attribute is always safer than dumping everything into one stat and discovering forty hours later that you needed something else.]

=== Appearance and Name

The character creator is detailed. Take your time here — you will look at this character for dozens of hours.

Choose a name that fits Vlandian or Battanian naming conventions, or create your own. Vlandian names draw from medieval Frankish and Norman traditions — Aldric, Evran, Isidore, Seraphine. Battanian names echo Celtic and Gaelic roots — Brigid, Eogan, Niamh, Ruarc. Your name appears in diplomacy, battle reports, the encyclopedia, and every conversation in the game. Make it memorable. You can grow a beard later. The barber in town will handle that.

== Controls and UI

=== Basic Controls

Bannerlord uses a combination of directional input for combat and standard keyboard shortcuts for menus. The default keybindings are well-designed and worth learning rather than remapping immediately.

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto),
  [*Action*], [*Key*],
  [Movement], [W, A, S, D],
  [Attack / Fire], [Left Mouse Button],
  [Block / Aim], [Right Mouse Button + direction],
  [Kick], [E (tap)],
  [Interact / Mount / Dismount], [F],
  [Inventory], [I],
  [Character Screen], [C],
  [Party Screen], [P],
  [Journal / Quests], [J],
  [Encyclopedia], [N],
  [Kingdom Screen], [K],
  [Clan Screen], [L],
  [Show Map Indicators], [Hold Alt],
  [Fast-Forward Time], [3],
  [Pause], [Space],
  [Troop Commands], [F1 through F7],
)

The encyclopedia (N) is your most powerful information tool. It contains every character, settlement, faction, and unit type in the game. Use it constantly — before accepting a quest, before entering a tournament, before attacking an enemy party. Knowledge is power, and the encyclopedia is free.

=== Combat Basics

Bannerlord's combat is directional. This is the defining feature of the series and what separates it from other action RPGs. Mastering it takes practice but rewards you with a combat system of unusual depth.

*Attacking.* Move your mouse in the direction you want to swing — left, right, up (overhead), or down (thrust) — then click the left mouse button. The direction of your swing determines its arc and the body part it targets. A quick flick produces a fast, light attack. Holding the mouse in the chosen direction builds power, increasing damage at the cost of speed and telegraphing. Learning when to commit is the core skill.

*Feinting.* Start a swing in one direction, then switch to another before releasing. Your opponent raises their guard to block the initial direction — you strike where they are no longer protecting. Feints work against all humanoid enemies, including lords and arena champions. They cost nothing and make the difference between winning and dying.

*Blocking.* Hold the right mouse button and match your attacker's weapon direction. Watch their weapon arm, not their face — the shoulder and elbow telegraph the incoming swing angle before the blade moves. Blocking is directional; holding the wrong guard gets you hit. This is why you practice on looters first.

*Chamber blocking.* Instead of blocking, attack into an incoming strike with a matching direction. This deflects their weapon and follows through with an immediate counterattack. High risk, devastating reward — a successful chamber block cannot be blocked or dodged. Start practicing this around hour ten, not hour one.

*Kicking.* Tap E while close to an enemy who is holding their block. The kick breaks their guard and staggers them for a free follow-up hit. The range is short — you must be nearly touching — and the timing is tight. Miss a kick and you give your opponent an opening.

#tip-box[Spend your first ten or fifteen minutes of any Bannerlord session in the Arena practice fights. The Arena uses equalized equipment and temporary knockouts instead of death, so you can experiment with every combat mechanic without consequence. Treat it like a warm-up — it will save your life on the real battlefield.]

=== Mounted Combat

Your horse is your greatest asset and your largest target. Mounted combat works by fundamentally different rules than fighting on foot, and understanding these differences is essential to survival.

Speed determines damage. A couched lance at full gallop — lowered and held steady, not swung — can one-shot nearly any enemy in the game. This is the cavalry's most iconic tool and your best argument for investing in a good horse early. A sword swung from a stationary horse does less damage than the same sword swung on foot with a running start. Momentum is everything.

Attack from the right side of your horse. The default camera favors right-side strikes, and most enemies will be on your right if you circle clockwise around formations. Attacking from the left requires awkward camera movement and costs you precious reaction time. Train a clockwise approach pattern — ride past the enemy on their left, your right — until it becomes muscle memory.

Never stop moving. A stationary mounted fighter is an easy target. Archers will pick you off, infantry will swarm you, and another rider will couch-lance you while you sit there. Keep circling. Use your horse's speed to dictate engagement range — close in to strike, pull away before they can respond.

Horses can die. Unlike companions, your mount does not come back. Purchase a backup horse and keep it in your inventory — when your primary goes down in battle, you can mount the spare. Nothing is worse than being unhorsed in the middle of a cavalry engagement with no way to remount.

#warning-box[Horse death is permanent. Your mount has its own health bar, and enemies target it deliberately — especially spearmen, who deal bonus damage to cavalry. If your horse goes down in the middle of an enemy formation, you are surrounded, on foot, and in serious trouble. Always carry a spare.]

=== Ranged Combat

Bows, crossbows, and throwing weapons each handle differently and fill distinct tactical roles. Your character's skill with each weapon type determines accuracy, damage, and handling — low skill means wide reticle bloom and sluggish aim.

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Weapon Type*], [*Characteristics*],
  [*Bow*], [Fastest rate of fire, moderate damage per arrow, best suited for horse archery. Requires the Bow skill, which governs accuracy and draw speed. High-skill archers can fire while backpedaling effectively. Ammo: quivers of 20-30 arrows.],
  [*Crossbow*], [Slowest reload but highest single-shot damage. Can be pre-loaded before combat and fired instantly on engagement. Can be used on horseback without the Bow skill, though accuracy suffers while moving. Ideal for the first volley before switching to melee. Ammo: bolts in stacks of 20.],
  [*Throwing Weapons*], [Shortest range, massive damage, severely limited ammunition — typically 3-5 javelins or axes. Devastating against shields (axes break them) and heavy armor (javelins punch through). Best used as an opening attack before closing to melee, or to finish fleeing enemies.],
)

Accuracy depends on your weapon skill level. At low skill, your reticle blooms wide and arrows fly unpredictably. At high skill, the reticle tightens faster and stays smaller during movement. Practice at the arena's archery range if available, or on looters before graduating to real battles. You do not need to be an expert archer to succeed — but having at least one ranged option for your party to use before the lines meet will win you fights before the swords come out.

== Your First Hour

The game drops you into the world with a handful of recruits, a little gold, and no direction. Here is what to do first.

=== Tutorial Quest

The main quest, "Investigate Neretzes' Folly," starts immediately. Ten lords across Calradia hold pieces of information about a battle that happened before your story began.

This quest spans the entire game. It is not urgent. Treat it as an excuse to explore the map and meet faction leaders — every noble you encounter during your travels becomes a potential conversation that incidentally completes an objective. Do not chase the quest markers. You will speak to lords naturally as you fight, trade, and seek mercenary contracts. The quest will complete itself over time.

=== Your First Recruits

Open your party screen by pressing P. This is your warband — your family, your security, and your largest expense. Every soldier here counts.

Recruit from villages for tier 1 troops and from towns for the occasional tier 2 soldier. Hover over a settlement on the world map to see which troop types it offers. Different cultures produce different unit trees — Vlandian villages produce crossbowmen and heavy cavalry, Battanian villages produce archers and skirmishers, and so on. Recruit where the troops you want are born.

Target a party size of 10 to 15 soldiers for your first week. This is enough to handle looters comfortably without wages crushing your finances. Composition matters more than raw numbers: aim for roughly 60% shielded infantry and 40% archers. The infantry holds a line. The archers kill from behind it. A pure infantry party takes unnecessary casualties from ranged enemies you could have thinned first. A pure archer party folds the moment the enemy closes to melee range.

#tip-box[Wages are paid daily. Every recruit costs money whether you fight or not. A party of 20 soldiers might cost 30-50 denars per day at tier 1, and that cost triples as they promote to tier 3 and beyond. Expand gradually. Never recruit soldiers you cannot afford to feed for a week without fighting — a bad streak of empty roads will bankrupt you.]

=== Food and Morale

Open your inventory and look at the food section. Every soldier you command eats every day. Run out of food and your army stops being an army.

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto, auto),
  [*Food*], [*Morale Units*], [*Notes*],
  [Grain], [12], [Cheapest bulk staple — buy in sacks, always carry at least a week's worth],
  [Meat], [8], [Moderate cost, decent sustenance — common in villages with hunters],
  [Fish], [6], [Cheap in coastal towns, expensive inland — buy where it is local],
  [Cheese], [5], [Moderate cost, moderate value — often available in highland villages],
  [Butter], [4], [Moderate cost, provides a morale boost when carried alongside other food types],
  [Grapes / Olives], [3], [Local specialty prices — buy in the regions where they grow],
)

The variety bonus is critical. Carrying three or more different food types grants a morale boost to your entire party. A bag of grain and a slab of meat will keep your soldiers alive, but add some butter and cheese and they will be happy about it. Happy soldiers fight better and are less likely to desert if things go wrong.

#danger-box[Zero food means starvation. Starvation leads to wounded troops, wounded troops lead to slowed movement on the world map, slowed movement means you cannot escape bandits, and bandits lead to a dead party. The cascade from "I forgot to buy grain" to "game over" can happen in under two minutes of world map travel. Check your food supply before leaving every town. Always carry a minimum of three days' worth.]

=== Your First Fight

Find looters on the world map. They travel in small bands of five to fifteen, are the weakest enemy in the game, and exist purely for you to practice on.

When you approach a looter party, the pre-battle screen offers two options. "Send Troops" runs an autoresolve calculation — at party parity, you will usually win, and your soldiers will gain experience without you risking a single personal swing. Use this for your very first fight to see how the system works and collect your first loot without pressure.

Your second fight should be manual. Select "Attack" and lead your soldiers into battle personally. The battlefield loads with your troops arrayed around you. Press F1 to bring up the movement command and click on the ground to position your troops, or press F1 then F3 to order a charge — your soldiers will seek out enemies and fight independently. For your first real battle, position your infantry on high ground with archers behind them and let the looters come to you. Charging downhill amplifies your soldiers' damage. Standing in a river slows you down.

Stay with your infantry line. A lone exile charging ten looters alone will be surrounded, stunned, and beaten to death regardless of your combat skill. Bannerlord is not a solo power fantasy — you are a commander first and a fighter second. Lead from within the formation, fight alongside your soldiers, and retreat if the line breaks.

Loot everything. Weapons, armor, horses, grain — take it all. Even junk sells for a few denars at the nearest town. Prisoners can be ransomed at taverns. Every looter fight is an economic transaction as much as a military one. The gold from selling loot funds your next round of recruitment and food.

=== Visit a Town

Enter the nearest town and walk its streets. The town interior is a navigable environment, not a menu, and each district holds different opportunities.

*The Tavern.* Your most important stop. Here you will find mercenaries for hire, companions to recruit, and a tavernkeeper who sells trade rumors and information. Sit down at a table. Look at the characters wandering the room — a name with a colored background denotes a companion or notable character you can speak to and potentially recruit.

*The Market.* Buy and sell goods. Prices vary by town based on local production and demand. A mining town sells ore cheap and buys tools at a premium. A farming village has cheap grain but needs iron. Learning these relationships is the foundation of profitable trading. The trade screen highlights prices in green (below average), white (average), and red (above average) so you can make informed decisions at a glance.

*The Arena.* This is your training ground. Arena practice matches use equalized equipment — everyone fights with the same tier of weapon and armor, regardless of what you own. Knockouts are temporary, and death is disabled entirely. Spend at least twenty minutes here before you fight any real battle. The Arena also hosts tournaments, which are competitive multi-round melees with a prize. Check the prize before entering — if it is a named weapon, a noble horse, or a piece of high-tier armor, the entry fee is worth paying.

*The Keep.* If the town's ruling noble is present, you will find them in the keep. This is where diplomatic conversations, quest hand-ins, and faction business happen. Visit the keep when you have business with a lord or want to ask for a mercenary contract.

#tip-box[Tournament prizes can be worth thousands of denars. A noble horse sells for more than most early-game trading runs earn in a week. Before entering any tournament, talk to the arena master and check the prize. If you see a named weapon like "Early Retirement" or a noble mount like a "Cossian," register immediately — even if you lose, the experience is valuable. If you win, the prize can fund your entire early game.]

== Building Your Warband

=== Party Composition

Your warband is your instrument. How you compose it determines how you fight, what enemies you can handle, and what tactics are available to you.

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Role*], [*Guidelines*],
  [*Shield Infantry (40%)*], [The backbone of your line. Shield infantry holds position, absorbs enemy charges, and protects your archers. Without shields, a formation crumbles under arrow fire before the melee even starts. Recruit from factions that produce heavy infantry — Vlandia for sergeants, Sturgia for axemen, the Empire for legionaries.],
  [*Archers (35%)*], [Your primary damage dealers. Archers force the enemy to advance into your infantry line or die standing still. Battanian Fian Champions are the best archers in the game, but every faction fields competent ranged troops. Position them on elevation behind your infantry for clear sightlines.],
  [*Cavalry (15%)*], [Your hammer. Cavalry flanks the enemy line, chases fleeing routers, and kills exposed archers. Vlandian knights and Khuzait lancers are top-tier, but even basic horsemen will route looters. Cavalry is expensive — both in recruitment cost and wages — so ease into it.],
  [*Horse Archers (10%)*], [Your harassment and skirmishing element. Horse archers circle enemy formations, forcing them to divide attention between the infantry in front and the arrows coming from the side. Khuzaits are the natural source, but the Aserai also field capable mounted archers. In Wave 0 this is optional; most early enemies do not demand this level of tactical sophistication.],
)

The principle is simple: shields hold, archers kill, cavalry exploits. A party that follows this ratio can handle any equal-sized enemy without needing advanced tactics. As you climb the clan tiers and face larger forces, these percentages will shift — more cavalry for faster map movement, more archers for sieges where infantry bottlenecks at the walls — but the core structure holds for your entire campaign.

=== Companions

Named characters you recruit from taverns. Unlike regular soldiers, companions do not permanently die — they are wounded and incapacitated but always recover. This alone makes them priceless. A companion you have invested in for fifty hours does not vanish to a lucky crossbow bolt.

Assign companions to roles through the Clan screen (L), under the Roles tab. Each role uses the companion's relevant skill, and a higher skill means better performance:

- *Scout* — increases map visibility radius and track detection, letting you spot enemy parties and avoid ambushes before they happen. Governed by Scouting.
- *Surgeon* — reduces the number of troops who permanently die after battle and speeds up wound recovery. Governed by Medicine.
- *Quartermaster* — increases party size capacity and makes food last longer. Governed by Steward.
- *Engineer* — speeds up siege engine construction and repair during sieges. Governed by Engineering.

Check a companion's stats before recruiting them. The tavernkeeper can tell you who is available, and the companion's character screen shows their skill numbers. Do not hire the first companion you find — travel to a few towns, compare candidates, and choose the one whose skills align with the role you need most urgently. A companion with 80 Medicine will save more of your troops than ten extra soldiers.

#tip-box[Companions can lead their own parties starting at Clan Tier 2. You send them out as independent warband leaders who recruit, fight, and earn income on their own — and they cost you nothing. This is covered in detail in Wave 1, but remember: every companion you hire now is a future general. Choose wisely.]

=== Equipment and Upgrades

Your personal equipment determines your combat effectiveness, and the upgrade priority is straightforward: weapon first, then armor, then horse, then shield. Do not buy the best item in each category immediately — upgrade progressively as your income allows.

A mid-tier one-handed sword and a decent chest armor beats a legendary blade and no body protection every time. You cannot deal damage while dead. Check the market in towns with smithies for better weapons, and towns near active war zones for discounted armor — fighting generates loot, and merchants near the front lines sell surplus from battles at lower prices.

Troops upgrade automatically. When a soldier earns enough experience in battle, an upgrade icon appears next to their name in the party screen — click it to promote them to the next tier. Higher-tier troops are dramatically better. A single tier 5 cavalry unit can rout a dozen looters without breaking a sweat. A tier 5 archer can drop enemies before they reach your line. Invest in your troops by fighting regularly — the experience they earn through combat is more valuable than the gold you spend on their wages.

== Trading 101

Trading is the simplest way to earn gold early. The principle is ancient: buy where goods are cheap, sell where they are expensive. Learning the specific relationships between towns is where the money is.

=== Understanding Prices

Hover over any trade good in the market screen to see its base price and compare the local price against it. Green text means the good is below average price — buy here. White text means average — avoid unless you have cargo space to fill. Red text means above average — sell here.

Every town consumes and produces. A village with iron mines sells iron ore at a fraction of the price you would pay in a city. That same city, if it has a smithy, buys iron ore at a premium because local demand exceeds local supply. A fishing village on the coast has cheap fish. An inland desert town pays triple for fish because it has none. Learning these production-and-demand relationships is the entire art of trading, and every profitable route you discover makes the next one easier to predict.

=== Trade Rumors

Visit any tavern and speak to the tavernkeeper. Pay the small fee for trade rumors and the keeper will update your market knowledge with current prices at nearby settlements. This sounds minor but is actually one of the best investments in the early game.

Trade rumors save you travel time. Without them, you might spend a day riding to a town that looked promising from the encyclopedia, only to discover the price has shifted and the deal is gone. With rumors, you know before you leave whether the journey is worth the saddle time. The fee is trivial compared to the opportunity cost of wasted travel.

=== Quick Profit Routes

These five routes are reliable for starting traders and do not require region-spanning journeys through dangerous territory.

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Route*], [*Details*],
  [Hardwood (Battania)], [Buy hardwood in Seonon, where Battanian logging villages produce it cheap. Sell in Marunath or Car Banseth, where limited local wood supply drives prices up. Short route, fast turnover.],
  [Grain (Vlandia)], [Buy grain in Vlandian farming villages — the western plains are Calradia's breadbasket. Sell in Sargot or Jaculan, port cities whose populations outstrip local food production. Always a demand-side market.],
  [Iron Ore (Vlandia)], [Buy iron ore in Pravend or Ocs Hall, where local mines saturate the market. Sell to any town with a smithy — the workshop consumes ore daily and pays premium prices for resupply.],
  [Fish (Coastal to Inland)], [Buy fish in coastal towns, especially in Vlandia and the western Empire, where it is a staple commodity. Sell inland and especially in desert towns — Aserai cities pay double or triple for seafood that does not exist in the desert.],
  [Dates (Aserai to North)], [Buy dates in Aserai towns, where desert groves produce them in surplus. Sell in northern towns — Sturgia and Battania have no native date production and wealthy nobles who will pay for luxury imports. High margin, longer route, worth the trip.],
)

#tip-box[Carry your trade goods on pack animals. Mules and sumpter horses are not glamorous, but they increase your carrying capacity from roughly 20 units to over 100. A single sumpter horse costs less than the profit from one full-load trading run. Buy one as soon as you can afford it — the capacity pays for itself on the first trip.]

=== Avoiding Bandits While Trading

The world map between towns is dangerous. Looters are slow and easily outrun, but the real threats are Forest Bandits, Sea Raiders, and Mountain Bandits — fast, well-equipped, and lethal to a small trading party.

Take the long safe road, not the forest shortcut. Time is cheap. Troops are expensive. A route that adds half a day to your journey but keeps you on open plains where bandit parties are visible from a distance will save your warband from a fight you cannot win. Travel at night when bandit activity decreases and most hostile parties move slower on the world map. Keep your party size above 15 before attempting cross-region routes — bandits evaluate your strength and will chase weak parties they sense they can defeat.

Save your game before entering known danger zones such as forest passes or mountain crossings. If you see a fast-moving red party icon changing course toward you, reload and reroute. The game does not punish you for caution.

#warning-box[Sea Raiders patrol the northern coasts and are among the deadliest bandits in the early game. They carry throwing weapons, wear chainmail, and move faster than Forest Bandits. If you are trading along the Sturgian coast or the northern Empire, give Sea Raider icons a wide berth. Do not fight them until your party is tier 3 or higher and you have shields on every infantry unit.]

== Clan Progression

Your clan tier determines your party size limit, companion party limit, and eligibility for mercenary contracts and vassalage. Climbing the clan tiers is the primary measure of progress in Bannerlord.

=== Renown

Renown is earned by winning battles against opponents who test you. The calculation is straightforward: the harder the fight, the more renown you gain.

Fighting looters produces negligible renown. A 15-man bandit band defeated by your 15-man warband earns almost nothing — you outmatched them and the world knows it. Fighting an enemy party equal to or stronger than yours earns significant renown. Winning against the odds — being outnumbered — applies a multiplier. A 20-man lord party defeated by your 15 soldiers earns substantial renown and respect. The same lord party defeated by your 50-man army earns far less.

#tip-box[Fight enemies your size or slightly larger for the best renown-to-time ratio. Crushing 30 looters with a 15-man warband is a waste of time for renown purposes, though the loot and prisoner ransom may still make it worthwhile economically. Check the pre-battle screen for the estimated renown reward before committing.]

=== Clan Tier Breakpoints

Each tier unlocks new capabilities and higher limits. Progress through them is the backbone of your campaign.

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto),
  [*Tier*], [*Requirements and Effects*],
  [*Tier 0*], [0 renown. Party size 20. Starting tier for all characters.],
  [*Tier 1*], [50 renown. Party size 40. Unlocks mercenary contracts — you can fight for any faction as a paid soldier of fortune.],
  [*Tier 2*], [150 renown. Party size 60. Unlocks companion parties — your companions can lead independent warbands. Factions will offer you vassalage. This is your Wave 0 goal.],
  [*Tier 3*], [350 renown. Party size 80. Unlocks kingdom policies and additional political influence. This is a stretch goal for Wave 0 — reachable with consistent play.],
  [*Tier 4*], [900 renown. Party size 110. Unlocks a second companion party. You are now a significant force on the map.],
)

For Wave 0, reaching Clan Tier 2 is your primary objective. At Tier 2, the entire game opens up — you can become a vassal, send companions out to build their own forces, and participate in faction politics. Tier 3 is a stretch goal for players who take to the game quickly. Anything beyond Tier 3 in Wave 0 is exceptional.

=== Mercenary Contracts

At Clan Tier 1, the hiring market opens. Visit any faction lord — approach them on the world map or find them in a town's keep — and ask to be hired as a mercenary. This is the safest way to earn steady income in the early game.

As a mercenary, the faction pays you wages based on your influence, which you earn by fighting their enemies. The faction partially subsidizes your troop wages — not enough to cover everything, but enough to keep a modestly-sized warband profitable while you fight. You gain access to the faction's towns for recruitment and trade, expanding your options beyond neutral settlements. And critically, you can leave the contract at any time with only a small relation penalty, freeing you to take a better offer when a different faction goes to war and starts paying more.

The mercenary loop is simple: sign with a faction at war, fight their enemies, collect influence and loot, leave when peace breaks out or a better-paying faction calls. Rinse and repeat until you have the gold, renown, and party strength to consider vassalage. You are a sword for hire. Act like one — loyalty is for lords, not mercenaries.

== Saving and Difficulty

=== Save Management

Bannerlord offers two save modes. *Free Save* (Normal) lets you save anytime, any number of files, and reload at will. Use this for Wave 0 — you are learning, and the game should not punish experimentation with permanent consequences. *Ironman* is a single save file that overwrites itself, and character death is permanent. Consider this in Wave 2, when you know the game well enough to accept the stakes.

#tip-box[Save before entering towns, before starting battles, and before approaching large parties on the world map. The game autosaves, but not at a frequency that will save you from a bad decision made in the thirty seconds between the last autosave and the Sea Raider ambush. Manual saving is free — use it.]

=== Difficulty Settings

Bannerlord's difficulty is granular. You can adjust individual sliders rather than choosing a single preset, and the right settings for Wave 0 are not the right settings for later waves.

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Setting*], [*Wave 0 Recommendation*],
  [*Player Received Damage*], [Reduced to 50% — you are learning combat and will make mistakes. Reduced damage means you survive them and learn from them instead of watching a loading screen.],
  [*Troop Received Damage*], [Realistic — your troops should feel valuable. If they die easily, you learn to protect them. Reduced troop damage teaches bad habits that will get your soldiers killed in later waves.],
  [*Recruitment Difficulty*], [Normal — standard troop availability at villages and towns. Easy recruitment floods the market with high-tier soldiers, which makes the game trivially simple.],
  [*Map Movement Speed*], [Realistic — accurate world map travel speeds. Your choices about routes and encumbrance matter.],
  [*Combat AI Difficulty*], [Normal — enemies fight competently but not at their maximum skill ceiling. Good difficulty factors in the enemy's combat skill, blocking, and feinting.],
  [*Persuasion Success*], [Normal — speech checks are not guaranteed. Your Charm skill and choices matter.],
  [*Clan Member Death*], [Reduced — companions are investments. A companion who dies to a random arrow after fifty hours of development is not a fun learning experience. Reduce death chance so you can grow attached to your companions without constant grief.],
)

#warning-box[These settings are for Wave 0 only. Wave 1 expects Realistic player damage, and Wave 2 expects maximum difficulty across the board. The ramp is intentional — you are meant to grow into the challenge. If you play Wave 0 on Realistic player damage and enjoy it, that is fine too. These are recommendations, not requirements.]

== Early Quests and Notables

=== Village Notables

Every village on the map contains named NPCs called notables — the headman, the local smith, a trader, and so on. These characters are your connection to the settlement's economy and troop pool.

Completing quests for notables increases your relation with them. Higher relation unlocks better recruits — at high relation, a village that used to offer only tier 1 peasants will produce tier 2 and occasionally tier 3 soldiers for you to recruit. Higher relation also improves trade prices in that village, giving you better margins on goods you buy and sell there. Invest time in villages whose troop trees you want to draw from. A few hours spent doing quests for a Battanian village whose archers you intend to recruit for the rest of the game pays dividends for the entire campaign.

=== Common Early Quests

The following quests appear frequently in villages and towns during the early game. Each one teaches a different aspect of the game and rewards you appropriately for your skill level.

*Family Feud.* A notable asks you to escort them to a confrontation with a rival family. When you arrive, a persuasion check resolves the dispute — no combat required. You will likely fail the first check; this is normal and teaches you how the persuasion system works. Good Charm XP, no risk of death, and the escort travel teaches you routes between settlements.

*Extortion by Deserters.* A group of soldiers has deserted and is extorting a village. Track them on the world map and fight them. Deserters are tougher than looters — they have military equipment and some combat experience — but their numbers are small. Easy combat, good relation reward, and the loot from dead deserters is better than bandit trash.

*Escort Merchant Caravan.* A merchant needs protection from one town to another. Your party escorts the caravan, which moves at your speed. Ambushes can happen along the route. This quest combines trade route learning with combat — you are essentially being paid to do what you should already be doing. Take every escort quest whose destination aligns with your travel plans.

*Hunt Bandits.* A notable asks you to clear a bandit hideout nearby. Hideouts are lairs that spawn hostile parties on the world map. Clearing a hideout involves a small-squad nighttime raid — you and a handful of your best soldiers enter the camp and fight room by room. Good combat practice in confined spaces, which is a skill that matters in sieges later. Renown for clearing the hideout, relation for the quest completion.

*Army of Poachers.* Poachers are operating near a village and the headman wants them gone. This quest introduces you to enemies who are not bandits — poachers are technically civilians, and how you handle them (combat versus persuasion) affects your reputation differently. Introduces you to the concept that not every hostile encounter is a simple "fight or flee" decision.

#tip-box[Accept all available quests before leaving a village, then execute them as a chain. Do the escort from Village A to Town B, stop in Town B to sell loot, then return to Village A with the quest completion and pick up the next one. Chaining quests by region keeps you in familiar territory where you know the towns, the markets, and the bandit spawns. Efficiency compounds — a smooth quest loop earns more gold and renown per hour than wandering the map looking for adventure.]

== Next Steps

Once you have completed a few quest loops, upgraded your troops to tier 2 and tier 3, and established a modest income through trading or mercenary work, you are ready for the *Getting Started* section. There you will set up your Wave 0 mods, configure your game, and begin a dedicated campaign with purpose and direction.

The road is long. The mountains are high. But you have taken the first step — and that is the hardest one.
